   "why": "32 positions of a black 2x2 box on a static 253-colour hue sweep, one circle of radius 5 about the centre, so the box advances roughly one pixel per frame. Each position is a distinct frozen image with its own hashes; the run repeats the loop 16 times. Distinctness is verified: 32 unique packet hashes."
  },
  "measurements_required": [
   "achieved inter-frame interval over 1024 frames -- this IS the device's ceiling for this shape",
   "ACK latency distribution and, critically, whether it rises across the run",
   "interval drift quarter by quarter, compared against R4's flat 130.5/128.5/132.4/132.7 ms",
   "any missing or malformed ACK",
   "operator observation: is the motion still smooth, or does it stutter or tear at this rate?",
   "operator observation: does the unit warm noticeably with zero idle time?"
  ],
  "automatic_retry": false,
  "automatic_reconnect": false,
  "target_override": false,
  "raw_packet_override": false
 },
 "budgets": {
  "connection_attempts": 1,
  "application_requests": 1024,
  "application_packets": 3072,
  "application_tx_bytes_total": 1073152,
  "expected_rx_frames": 1024,
  "expected_rx_bytes_total": 10240,
  "send_spacing_ms": 0,
  "inter_frame_delay_ms": 0,
  "ack_timeout_ms_per_frame": 5000,
  "total_wall_clock_ms": 150000,
  "derivation": "1024 frames at a predicted ~61 ms is about 63 s, matching R4's duration so the two are comparable as endurance tests. Wall clock is 150 s, well over double, so a run that turns out SLOWER than predicted is measured rather than truncated -- discovering that the device needs the idle time is a legitimate and interesting outcome."
 },
 "expected_observations": {
  "transport": "one connection, 3072 packets, 1024 wrapped 0x44 ACKs, clean close after about 63 s",
  "physical": "the black box circling roughly twice as fast as R4, thirty-two laps in about a minute",
  "ack_payload": "recorded per frame, not asserted"
 },
 "persistence_analysis": {
  "expected_effect": "display state only",
  "basis": "M6.4 power cycle returned the unit to stock software and the default clock",
  "not_claimed": "absence of any non-volatile write; repetition does not test that either",
  "new_persistence_risk_vs_step_1": "none identified: identical command, identical packets, same session shape, repeated. No new command id, no save/publish/upload, no gallery interaction.",
  "new_risk_vs_previous": "Frame SIZE is new; the command, packet structure, target, rate and session shape are unchanged. No new command id, no save/publish/upload, no gallery interaction. The encoder's 255-colour ceiling and the existing frame-size guard both still apply."
 },
 "stop_policy": {
  "stop_immediately_on": [
   "unexpected target identity or pairing precheck failure",
   "any ACK that is not exactly one 10-byte wrapped 0x44 frame",
   "any send that does not complete in full",
   "ACK timeout on any frame",
   "total wall clock exceeding 30000 ms",
   "any conflicting controller observed on the link",
   "a stock page takeover or any device behaviour outside the expected observations"
  ],
  "on_ambiguous_outcome": "stop, preserve the Host diagnostics record including the per-frame timings collected so far, and report. Do NOT resend, do not reconnect, do not power-cycle before the result is recorded.",
  "recovery": "operator-initiated only, after the outcome is recorded",
  "operator_abort": "the run is short enough to watch end to end; if it looks wrong, power off the Ditoo rather than letting it continue."
 },
 "implementation_gate": {
  "host_change_required": "MinInterFrameDelayMs 50 -> 0 and MaxSequenceFrames 512 -> 1024",
  "status": "built, installed and verified 2026-09-09. Installed DLL c714fd37492ff371587576e8bc7b70c608592e254186332d66cfa3c8e58b4859, byte-identical to the repository build, REFRESH_STATUS=PASS_TYPED_IMAGE, OPENTIVOO_TASK=preserved, installed binary passes the shared receive fixture.",
  "note": "0 is the floor for both values -- there is nothing below it. With no sleeps the transport performs no waiting of its own; it blocks only on the ACK event."
 },
 "authority": {
  "transmission_authorized": false,
  "authorization_consumed": false,
  "experiment_id": "OPENDITOO-R5-NOSLEEP-CEILING-001",
  "granted_by": null,
  "grant_text": null,
  "expires_at": null,
  "grant_scope_requested": "ONE execution: 1024 frames, 3072 packets, 1073152 application bytes, about 63 seconds continuous with zero inter-frame delay and zero send spacing, one connection, no retry, no reconnect. Not continuous or unattended display, not pipelining.",
  "note": "Grants nothing as written."
 },
 "explicitly_out_of_scope": [
  "pipelining or any change to the one-ACK-per-frame shape -- that is the only way past this ceiling and it is not in scope",
  "runs longer than 1024 frames, or keeping the connection open beyond this run",
  "any new command id, delta-frame or partial-update command, or command enumeration",
  "driving frames from the M9 activity collector"
 ],
 "build": {
  "host_dll_sha256": "c714fd37492ff371587576e8bc7b70c608592e254186332d66cfa3c8e58b4859",
  "installed_dll_sha256_verified": "c714fd37492ff371587576e8bc7b70c608592e254186332d66cfa3c8e58b4859"
 },
 "why_this_value_matters": "Every other timing number in this project was chosen by us and has proven reducible: the inter-frame delay went 1000 -> 250 -> 50 ms with no ill effect. The 40 ms send spacing is the ONLY timing taken from observed stock-app behaviour, so it is the only one that might encode a real device requirement.",
 "why_this_shape": "The operator's point: alternating full-screen images cannot show smoothness. A small object with continuous motion can -- a 2x2 box moving roughly one pixel per frame around a circle makes choppiness immediately obvious, and a black box on a rainbow sweep is maximally legible. Content stays at the 255-colour ceiling (1033 B images) so this remains a full-colour streaming test rather than a cheap one.",
 "the_real_risk": {
  "exposure": "1024 frames, 1,073,152 application bytes, about 63 seconds, running the device flat out with zero idle time between frames. R4 was the same duration at half the rate and with 70 ms of deliberate idle per frame; this removes all of it.",
  "why_the_device_cannot_be_overrun": "Every frame still waits for its own ACK before the next begins, so the device self-paces -- it cannot fall behind a producer that only advances when it answers. This is why 'no sleep' is safe to attempt at all: it is not a flood, it is the removal of artificial idling.",
  "unknowns": [
   "whether sustained zero-idle operation heats the unit or provokes throttling",
   "whether ACK latency rises when the device gets no gap between frames",
   "whether the display can actually repaint at this rate or begins to tear"
  ],
  "why_it_is_still_bounded": "Any malformed or missing ACK, incomplete send, ACK timeout or the wall-clock ceiling stops the run with no retry and no reconnect. Worst case is a stopped experiment.",
  "operator_abort": "power the unit off if it looks wrong or feels hot; a stopped run is a recorded result."
 },
 "why_this_is_the_right_shape": "We cannot go faster than this without abandoning one ACK per frame, which is a different protocol shape and is not in scope. So this is not one more step on a ladder: it is the LAST step the current shape admits. Everything below is the device's own pace plus our loop overhead.\n\nIt deliberately changes TWO values at once -- delay 50 -> 0 and spacing 10 -> 0 -- which breaks the one-variable discipline every earlier rate run held to. That is justified here because the question is not 'what does each wait cost' (R2a and R2b already answered that) but 'where is the wall'. Attribution is not the goal; the ceiling is. If it degrades, the follow-up bisects: delay 0 with spacing 10 is the obvious next probe.",
 "predicted": {
  "from_r4_decomposition": "R4 sustained 131.0 ms = 50 ms delay + 20 ms spacing + 44 ms real send/ACK + 18 ms loop overhead. Removing both waits leaves about 61 ms.",
  "interval_ms": "roughly 61 ms, about 16 fps",
  "what_would_falsify_it": "An interval materially above ~80 ms would mean the device slows when denied breathing room -- that the waits we removed were doing something after all. That is a genuine possibility and is the main thing worth learning here.",
  "what_degradation_would_look_like": "ACK latency climbing across the run, interval drift between quarters, a missing or malformed ACK (which stops the run), or visible stutter, tearing or dropped motion. R4 showed none of these at 7.6 fps over 67 s; this asks the same question at roughly twice the rate."
 },
 "result": null
}
